<ul>
<li>TLS tunnel: a protected connection between two endpoints; here each hop (client→provider, provider→recipient) is its own separate tunnel.</li>
<li>ECDHE / forward secrecy: the ephemeral key exchange that means past sessions stay safe if the long-term key is later stolen — that's the &quot;forward secrecy&quot; tag on the tunnel. But note it only protects <em>in transit</em>.</li>
<li>The two-hop detail is the whole lesson: the tunnel stops at your provider, so your provider reads the body <em>and</em> headers in plaintext (the Note-over-P red flag). A second tunnel doesn't help because the message is re-readable at the relay.</li>
<li>at rest: mail stored unencrypted on disk by the provider — &quot;readable at rest&quot; is the failure mode TLS never addresses.</li>
<li>Why this matters: most users assume &quot;encrypted&quot; = safe; this slide shows TLS alone leaves the provider holding everything.</li>
</ul>
