<ul>
<li>tbsCertificate: &quot;to-be-signed certificate&quot; — the structured part (subject, public key, SAN, EKU) that the CA actually signs. The signature is over this body, which is why tampering with it breaks the signature.</li>
<li>signed public key / signatureValue: a cert is literally a public key plus an authority's signature over it — it <em>never</em> contains the private key, ever.</li>
<li>Subject Alternative Name (SAN): the field that binds the cert to a specific mailbox, here <code>email:alice@example.com</code>. The verifier checks the sender's From: against this — a cert for the wrong address fails at this step.</li>
<li>Extended Key Usage (EKU): says what the cert is <em>allowed</em> to do — here <code>id-kp-emailProtection</code>, i.e. &quot;this cert is for email.&quot; The OID <code>1.3.6.1.5.5.7.3.4</code> is the exact identifier; the ⚠️ is because it's easy to mis-write (some docs use the wrong trailing <code>.12</code>).</li>
<li>OID: Object Identifier — a globally unique dotted number that names a thing (here: a usage policy). <code>.3.4</code> = emailProtection; getting it wrong breaks S/MIME validation.</li>
<li>Key Usage: a stricter field limiting what the key may be used for — <code>digitalSignature</code> (signing) and <code>keyEncipherment</code> (wrapping a session key). The two distinct jobs the key is allowed to do.</li>
<li>Intuition: a certificate is like an ID card — it proves the <em>public</em> key belongs to Alice and is only for email; the private half is never on the card.</li>
</ul>
